  <section id="summary">
    <article class="overview">
      <p class="blog-lead-paragraph">I joined JustRelate in December 2024 as Head of Design, stepping into a role that was equal parts exciting and challenging. JustRelate had a solid product suite and a strong customer base of 4,000+ companies — but the different products had evolved somewhat independently over time, making it difficult for both users and the company to see (and communicate) the full picture.</p>
      <p class="blog-lead-paragraph">My remit was broad from day one: define a product vision that tied everything together, build a unified design practice from a team that had never really operated as one, and establish the design operations foundations that would allow us to scale. Oh, and there was also an AI layer to conceptualise. =P</p>
    </article>

    <article id="basics">
      <div class="section-table">
        <div class="section-table-cell">
          <h4 class="blog-text-3">Category</h4>
          <p class="blog-paragraph">B2B, SaaS, Design Operations, Design Leadership, AI</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-table-cell">
          <h4 class="blog-text-3">Platforms</h4>
          <p class="blog-paragraph">Web</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-table-cell">
          <h4 class="blog-text-3">Role</h4>
          <p class="blog-paragraph">Head of Design</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-table-cell">
          <h4 class="blog-text-3">Time</h4>
          <p class="blog-paragraph">Dec 2024 – present</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <hr class="bold-divider">

  <section id="context">
    <h2 class="blog-text-2">Context</h2>

    <article>
      <h2 class="blog-text-3">About JustRelate</h2>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">JustRelate builds software for Marketing, Sales, and Service teams. The flagship product, JustRelate Home, is a unified platform that brings together websites, emails, CRM, automation, CPQ, and service processes — all in one place, all aimed at helping companies build better, longer-lasting customer relationships.</p>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">The platform is trusted by 4,000+ companies, with a particularly strong presence in the DACH region. Beyond Home, JustRelate offers a family of specialised Builders — tools for creating and managing digital experiences — which together with Home form the complete JustRelate platform.</p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2 class="blog-text-3">Problem definition</h2>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">The challenge wasn't that JustRelate had bad products — quite the opposite. But each product had evolved with a degree of independence, which over time created friction:</p>
      <ul class="blog-ul">
        <li class="blog-paragraph">From a <b>user perspective</b>, moving between products felt disconnected. Each had its own design language, interaction patterns, and mental model, creating unnecessary cognitive load.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph">From a <b>business perspective</b>, the lack of a clear platform narrative made it harder to position, sell, and communicate the full value of what JustRelate offered.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph">From a <b>design perspective</b>, there was no shared infrastructure — no design system, no shared component libraries, no defined processes. Designers were skilled, but working in silos.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2 class="blog-text-3">Objective</h2>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">Turn a collection of great products into a coherent platform — and build the design practice that could sustain that vision long-term.</p>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">This meant working on three parallel fronts: product vision, team unification, and design operations. Not the easiest starting point, but definitely the most interesting one. ;)</p>
    </article>

  </section>

  <section id="platform-vision">
    <h2 class="blog-text-2">Platform vision</h2>

    <article>
      <h3 class="blog-text-3">Reframing the product</h3>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">My first task was to make sense of the product landscape and find the narrative that could hold everything together. Rather than treating the different products as separate entities that happened to live under the same brand, the opportunity was to reframe them as a platform — with JustRelate Home at the centre, and the Builders orbiting around it as specialised, composable capabilities.</p>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">This reframing had implications beyond product design. It shaped how we talked about the platform internally and externally, how roadmaps were structured, and how teams thought about their work in relation to each other's.</p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3 class="blog-text-3">JustRelate Home as the platform hub</h3>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">JustRelate Home became the strategic centre of the platform vision — the place where all customer-facing activities converge. Marketing, Sales, and Service no longer needed to be experienced as separate silos; Home provides the unified layer that orchestrates all of them, giving users a single place to manage campaigns, customer data, pipelines, and service processes.</p>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">Defining what Home should be — and equally what it shouldn't try to be — was a key part of the early work. A platform hub needs clarity of purpose to avoid becoming everything for everyone and nothing for nobody.</p>
    </article>

  </section>

  <section id="jr-coworker">
    <h2 class="blog-text-2">JR Coworker</h2>

    <article>
      <h3 class="blog-text-3">Conceptualising the AI layer</h3>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">One of the most exciting challenges was thinking about how AI could be woven into the platform in a way that felt coherent and genuinely useful — rather than just bolting on AI features per product in an ad hoc way.</p>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">The concept I developed, JR Coworker, proposes a unified AI layer that ties together Home, the Builders, and the entire platform. Rather than having each product develop its own AI capabilities in isolation, JR Coworker acts as a shared intelligence layer: aware of context across the platform, able to assist users regardless of which tool they're in, and capable of automating and connecting workflows that previously required manual switching between products.</p>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">This kind of AI integration requires thinking holistically about the platform — which is exactly why having a unified design vision first was a prerequisite for making it work well.</p>
    </article>

  </section>

  <section id="design-team-designops">
    <h2 class="blog-text-2">Design team & DesignOps</h2>

    <article>
      <h3 class="blog-text-3">Building a team, not just managing designers</h3>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">When I arrived, JustRelate had designers — but not really a design team. People were doing good work, but without a shared practice: no common processes, no shared tools philosophy, no regular design critique or cross-pollination. Building the team meant creating the conditions for that to change.</p>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">The goal was to foster a culture of ownership, shared standards, and mutual feedback — where designers felt part of a coherent practice, not just embedded in their respective product areas. This is an ongoing effort, but the shift has been noticeable.</p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3 class="blog-text-3">DesignOps from zero</h3>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">There was essentially no design operations infrastructure when I joined. No defined handoff process, no component libraries, no documented design principles, no onboarding for new designers. Everything was in people's heads or scattered across files.</p>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">Building DesignOps from scratch is both daunting and hugely satisfying. I started by identifying the highest-friction points — the places where the lack of process was costing the most time and quality — and worked outward from there:</p>
      <ul class="blog-ul">
        <li class="blog-paragraph"><b>Design principles</b> — giving the team a shared language for design decisions and critique.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph"><b>Component library foundations</b> — establishing the groundwork for a design system that can grow with the platform.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph"><b>Processes and rituals</b> — introducing regular design reviews, design critiques, and clearer handoff workflows with engineering.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph"><b>Tooling alignment</b> — standardising the tools the team uses so collaboration doesn't require constant context switching.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

  </section>

  <hr class="bold-divider">

  <section id="wrap-up">
    <h2 class="blog-text-2">Wrap up</h2>

    <article>
      <h3 class="blog-text-3">Early outcomes</h3>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">Seven months in, this is very much a work in progress — but the direction is set and momentum is building:</p>
      <ul class="blog-ul">
        <li class="blog-paragraph">A clear <b>platform vision centred on JustRelate Home</b> is now shared across product, design, and leadership — shaping roadmap conversations and go-to-market messaging.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph">The <b>JR Coworker concept</b> has been well received internally and is being worked into the product strategy as the AI layer for the platform.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph">The <b>design team is operating as a team</b> — with shared rituals, clearer ownership, and a growing sense of common identity and craft.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph">The foundations of a <b>DesignOps practice</b> are in place, making onboarding, collaboration, and consistency meaningfully easier than they were.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3 class="blog-text-3">What's next</h3>
      <p class="blog-paragraph">There's plenty still ahead:</p>
      <ul class="blog-ul">
        <li class="blog-paragraph"><b>Design system</b> — moving from component library foundations to a full, documented design system that product teams can rely on.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph"><b>JR Coworker</b> — taking the concept from vision into product design and working with engineering to bring the AI layer to life.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph"><b>Platform cohesion</b> — continuing to close the experience gaps between Home and the Builders, making the platform feel truly unified from the user's perspective.</li>
        <li class="blog-paragraph"><b>Team growth</b> — scaling the design team and the DesignOps infrastructure to support the platform's ambitions.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

  </section>

